# Graph terms: quoting, unquoting and identity
#
# Bob says things about Alice, and everything Bob says is true; Lois Lane
# knows that Superman can fly, and Superman is Clark Kent; a graph term
# says that someone knows :p and that :p calls them a genius. eyemind
# concludes what Alice likes, that Lois Lane's knowledge stays about
# Superman, that renaming a graph term's own blank nodes keeps it the same
# graph term, and that everyone says of themselves that they are wise. It
# shows quoted formulas as opaque graph terms, matched as sets up to a
# one-to-one renaming of their own variables. The statements are the
# examples of D. Arndt and P.-A. Champin, "Notation3: Graph Terms for RDF"
# (arXiv:2610.31266), Equations 2, 5, 8, 9 and 26 and Section 4.4.

@prefix : <http://example.org/> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .

# Bob says that Alice likes pizza and pasta, and everything Bob says is true.
:bob :says { :alice :likes :pizza, :pasta } .
{ :bob :says ?x } => ?x .

# Whoever likes pasta also likes spaghetti.
{ ?x :likes :pasta } => { ?x :likes :spaghetti } .

# Graph terms are opaque: Superman is Clark Kent, but what Lois Lane knows
# is a statement about Superman.
:superman owl:sameAs :clarkKent .
:loisLane :knows { :superman :can :fly } .
{ :loisLane :knows { :clarkKent :can :fly } } => { :loisLane :knowsAbout :clarkKent } .
{ :loisLane :knows { :superman :can :fly } } => { :loisLane :knowsAbout :superman } .

# G1 of Section 4.4. Its blank nodes are its own: G2 renames them, and G3
# turns the constant :Genius into a blank node.
:g1 :is { _:x :knows :p . :p :says { _:x a :Genius } } .
{ :g1 :is { _:y :knows :p . :p :says { _:x a :Genius } } } => { :g1 :isomorphicTo :g2 } .
{ :g1 :is { _:y :knows :p . :p :says { _:x a _:z } } } => { :g1 :isomorphicTo :g3 } .

# Everyone says about themselves that they are wise: ?x belongs to the
# whole document, inside the graph term too.
?x :says { ?x :is :wise } .
{ :tom :says ?g } => { :tom :said ?g } .
